From 5cde814d2bf0dac1f97a14c1f40ea68674646a34 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: agent-keys <noreply@agent-keys.invalid>
Date: Sat, 28 Nov 2026 12:04:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/20] fix(operator): refuse a mandate that lets the agent
 withdraw the owner's grant to the Safe

The Safe's standing approval on the IdentityRegistry is the owner's root operation, and the
migration never sends it with the agent's permission. But an agent config whose mandate lists
(IdentityRegistry, setApprovalForAll) lets the agent call setApprovalForAll(Safe, false) alone:
AgentPolicy refuses an operation that leaves an operator grant open, not one that withdraws it,
and it cannot hold the call to its arguments. A point of #250 in the agent-keys repository (B2).

- signer/migrate.ts: assertMandateKeepsOwnersGrant refuses while the entry is in the agent
  config's policy.allow (what the owner's next rotation installs; the target compared as an
  address, the selector without regard to case) or admitted by the AgentPolicy installed for the
  permission on the Kernel (AgentPolicy.isAllowed(Kernel, permission, IdentityRegistry,
  setApprovalForAll), what binds the agent now). The error says where it found the entry, why
  it is refused, and that the owner rotates the permission to a mandate without it. The
  migration runs it in step 2 for a Safe with an ERC-8004 identity, in a dry run too, before
  anything changes hands; a Safe without an identity has no grant to keep. readAgentConfig reads
  the permission id and policy.allow of the agent config; installedPolicy is the policy lookup
  step 2 and the check share.
- main.ts: the boot runs the same check for each Safe of migrationTargets that has an identity.
- cli/commands/signer-migrate.ts: reads the agent config with readAgentConfig and passes it to
  the migration; the help says what is refused.
- tests: unit: the migration refuses the entry in the agent config (also written in lower and
  upper case), in a run and in a dry run, sending nothing, and a mandate on the Kernel that admits
  it whatever the config says; setApprovalForAll on another contract and other calls on the
  registry pass, and a Safe without an identity is not held to it; the boot's check with no Kernel
  code reads the config alone. In the full loop: the AgentPolicy on the chain does not admit the
  entry for the loop's mandate, so the check passes there, and the same config with the entry
  added is refused. Each rule was broken once on purpose and a test caught it (the config's
  entry, the installed mandate's, the step not calling the check, the selector's case).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
